1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,920
How does our system talk to Uzcard and Humo, when they speak different languages?

2
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:11,376
Uzcard speaks JSON-RPC, and Humo speaks SOAP, with different fields and different errors.

3
00:00:11,426 --> 00:00:19,128
If the payment code knew both, every new scheme would mean touching payment code, and no other project could reuse the work.

4
00:00:19,178 --> 00:00:27,648
So each scheme gets its own adapter: a separate Spring Boot service with its own OpenAPI contract, that any service or project may call.

5
00:00:27,698 --> 00:00:37,656
Every adapter answers plain REST: an envelope with a request id, the scheme's status and the data, or a problem detail that carries the scheme's own code.

6
00:00:37,706 --> 00:00:44,760
Inside, every scheme method is one command, picked by its type from a factory, the same way in both adapters.

7
00:00:44,810 --> 00:00:53,184
A status file in each adapter turns every scheme code into a status, and the card module turns the scheme's decline codes into our own.

8
00:00:53,234 --> 00:01:06,408
Uzcard debits in one step, by the card's token, with our payment id as the external reference; if that reference was sent before, Uzcard answers pending instead of paying twice, and a lookup by that reference settles it.

9
00:01:06,458 --> 00:01:13,992
Humo holds the money first, and the card module captures the hold right away; a capture that gets no answer is taken on the next lookup.

10
00:01:14,042 --> 00:01:21,072
The card module needs only the published client library and one port, a card processor, with one processor per scheme.

11
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:26,952
Every answer becomes one of three outcomes: approved, declined with our own code, or unknown.

12
00:01:27,002 --> 00:01:35,424
A timeout answers five oh two, and the card module reads it as unknown, never a decline, because the money may already have moved.

13
00:01:35,474 --> 00:01:39,552
Next, knowing a card's scheme, and storing its number safely.
